===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}} {{term-label|en|computing|humorous|informal|historical}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A [[method]] of [[transfer#Verb|transferring]] a [[computer]] [[file#Noun|file]] from one computer to another by [[copy#Verb|copying]] it to a [[floppy disk]], [[thumb drive]], or some other [[external]] [[storage]] [[device]], [[carry#Verb|carrying]] the device to the other computer, and [[save#Verb|saving]] the file there, in contrast to [[electronic]] methods used by [[networked#Adjective|networked]] computers to transfer [[data]]. {{defdate|from 1980s}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|magazine=Hardcopy|location=Brea, Calif.|publisher=Sedlin Pub. Co.|year=1986|volume=6|page=143|column=3|issn=0279-8123|oclc=479346585|passage=Significant advances are occurring to race past '''sneakernet'''. But the ideal computer interconnect solution will always be just around the next bend.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=David Plotkin|title=Compute! Specific|magazine={{w|Compute!}}|location=Greensboro, N.C.|publisher=COMPUTE! Publications, [[w:American Broadcasting Company|ABC Publishing]]|month=April|year=1989|volume=11|issue=4, issue 107|page=58|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/1989-04-compute-magazine/page/n59/mode/1up|column=2|issn=0194-357X|oclc=473378905|passage=Before complicated networks and E-mail and special-interest bulletin boards, there was '''Sneakernet'''. '''Sneakernet''' required no special protocol, no special cables and terminal software. To communicate on '''Sneakernet''', all you needed to do was take a disk from your computer to someone else's, stick the disk in that remote drive, and copy some files—instant data transfer. Well, almost instant.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Robin Anderson|author2=Andy Johnston [''et al.'']|chapter=File Sharing|title=Unix Unleashed|edition=4th|location=Indianapolis, Ind.|publisher={{w|Sams Publishing}}|month=December|year=2001|section=part II (Critical Subsystems)|page=546|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=pO6rRW2736AC&pg=PA546|isbn=978-0-672-32251-8|passage=Before computer networks came into existence, '''''sneakernet''''' was the  only method of file sharing available. '''Sneakernet''' is the generally accepted term for the act of loading files that you want to share on removable media (tape, CD, or floppy disk) at one computer, carrying the media to a second computer, and reading the files from that media onto that second computer. [...] '''Sneakernet''' is the lowest-tech form of file sharing that has ever been available, and it is still practiced at some sites today.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} The [[group#Noun|group]] of computers involved in this [[practice]].
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=An Introduction to the Internet for Financial Services Developers|editor=Jessica Keyes|title=Handbook of Technology in Financial Services|location=Boca Raton, Fla.; New York, N.Y.|publisher={{w|CRC Press}}|year=1999|year_published=2010|section=section I (Technology Trends in Financial Services)|page=!25-7|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=TWC1DwAAQBAJ&pg=SA25-PA7|isbn=978-0-8493-9981-7|passage=NSA feels there is no reason to use '''sneakernet''' anymore when you can distribute software to users through the network. ('''Sneakernet''' is the network created by physically walking from location to location to deliver software to users.)}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Doug Kaye|chapter=Application Integration|editors=Alison Bing and Cessna Kaye|title=Loosely Coupled: The Missing Pieces of Web Services|location=Marin County, Calif.|publisher=RDS Press|year=2003|section=part II (Concepts)|page=66|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=1VzEQoU1qMQC&pg=PA66|isbn=978-1-881378-24-2|passage=Some companies were able to coerce one department's application to read data files written by another's. These lucky organizations often resorted to '''''sneakernets'''''—transferring data on magnetic tape or floppy disks—to avoid the re-keying process. With the advent of LANs and WANs, electronic file transfers replaced most '''sneakernets'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Adrian W. Kingsley-Hughes|chapter=What’s What—the Anatomy of a PC|title=The PC Doctor’s Fix-it-yourself Guide|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:McGraw-Hill Education|McGraw-Hill]]|year=2004|section=part I (Overview)|page=24|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=wWaH01s8TF4C&pg=PA24|doi=10.1036/0072255536|isbn=978-0-07-225553-9|passage=Before the days of Internets and intranets, there were '''''sneakernets'''''—users would save files on a floppy disk and simply walk them over to another PC!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Jaroslav Švelch|chapter=Lighting up the Shadows: Informal Distribution of Game Software|title=Gaming the Iron Curtain: How Teenagers and Amateurs in Communist Czechoslovakia Claimed the Medium of Computer Games|location=Cambridge, Mass.; London|publisher={{w|MIT Press}}|year=2018|page=151|pageurl=https://books.google.comg/books?id=XWB9DwAAQBAJ&pg=PA151|isbn=978-0-262-03884-3|passage=Czechoslovak '''sneakernets''' were structured around the geopolitical barriers and foreign trade regulations, but without any direct intervention from the authorities. The networks were improvisational and unruly, but also robust and reliable. [...] '''Sneakernets''' were experienced as ''movement'' rather than as a service or a set of formalized transactions.}}

